explanations.E0062:
# E0062: optional used as if it were present

A field read, a method call, an index or a `for` needs a value, not an optional of one. An optional may be `None`, which has no fields, methods or elements.

## Why

Sisu has no null pointer errors at runtime: the checker makes the program say what happens when the optional is `None`. `?.` reads the member only when the optional holds a value, and gives `None` otherwise. In an assignment or a `push`, `?.` makes the change only when the optional holds a value, and does nothing otherwise. `if let` runs a block with the value only when it is present. `let ... else` and `?? return` unwrap the value and leave when it is `None`.

The checker first looks for the member on the value inside the optional, as `?.` does. When that value has no such member, as in `n.size()` on a `Node?` where `Node` has no `size`, or the member does not apply to it, the error is the one that `?.` gives, such as [E0063](E0063.md) or [E0058](E0058.md), not E0062: a `?.` would not fix it.

An assignment to an element, such as `m?.items[0] = 2`, writes into the array that `m?.items` holds. An `if let` of that array binds a copy, and you cannot write to a copy. The help names the object of the `?.` instead: `if let m = m { m.items[0] = 2 }` writes into the array of `m`.

A `push` onto an optional array, such as `xs.push(2)` on an `[i64]?`, gets no `if let` help, because an unwrapped array is a copy. The `?.` fix is the form that changes the array: `xs?.push(2)`. The help names the object of a `?.` only when that object stores the array, as in `bag?.items.push(4)`.

An optional of an optional, such as a `Node??`, has two layers, and `?.` takes only one off: `n?.value` on a `Node??` reads `value` from a `Node?`, which may be `None` too. Peel the other layer first with `??`: `(n ?? None)?.value`.

## Broken

```sisu
class Node {
    value: i64
    next: Node?
}

fn second(n: Node) -> i64 {
    n.next.value
}

fn main() {
    print(second(Node(value: 1, next: Node(value: 2, next: None))))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
class Node {
    value: i64
    next: Node?
}

fn second(n: Node) -> i64 {
    if let next = n.next {
        next.value
    } else {
        0
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(second(Node(value: 1, next: Node(value: 2, next: None))))
}
```

## Related

- [E0049](E0049.md): `?.` on a value that is not optional.

explanations.E0130:
# E0130: `match` that does not cover every value

A `match` needs an arm for every value its scrutinee can hold. When a value reaches no arm, the error names one such value as a pattern, at the `match` keyword: "`match` does not cover `Mul(_, _)`". When an arm has the missing value's variant at the top, a label on that arm says which part of the variant it covers: "`Add(Num(_), _)` covers only `Num(_), _`". When a guarded arm may not match the value, a label on the `match` keyword says so: a guarded arm covers nothing, since its guard can be false.

Integers and `char`s are finite, so literals and ranges that together hold every value cover the type: `0..=127` and `128..=255` cover a `u8`. A missing integer or `char` is named as the first gap from the bottom, "`match` does not cover `10..=255`", with the help "add `_ =>`"; so is a `string`, which only `_` or a name covers.

An optional has two values to cover: `None`, and `Some` of each value inside. A missing value of an optional is named as a pattern at the optional's position: "`match` does not cover `None`", "`match` does not cover `Some(None)`" in an optional of an optional, or the missing variant of the enum inside, "`match` does not cover `Square(_)`".

## Why

A `match` is an expression, so it must produce a value, or leave, for every input. Checking coverage when the program compiles turns a forgotten variant into an error at the `match`, instead of a crash when that value arrives. Rust and Swift reject such a `match` too. Adding a variant to an enum then points at every `match` that must handle it.

The unsafe fix adds `_ => todo` as the last arm. It is unsafe because a catch-all also takes every variant added later, which then goes unnoticed.

## Broken

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
    Add(Expr, Expr)
    Mul(Expr, Expr)
}

fn eval(e: Expr) -> i64 {
    match e {
        Num(n) => n
        Add(a, b) => eval(a) + eval(b)
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(eval(Expr.Add(Expr.Num(1), Expr.Num(2))))
    print(eval(Expr.Mul(Expr.Num(2), Expr.Num(3))))
}
```

## Fixed

```sisu
enum Expr {
    Num(i64)
    Add(Expr, Expr)
    Mul(Expr, Expr)
}

fn eval(e: Expr) -> i64 {
    match e {
        Num(n) => n
        Add(a, b) => eval(a) + eval(b)
        Mul(a, b) => eval(a) * eval(b)
    }
}

fn main() {
    print(eval(Expr.Add(Expr.Num(1), Expr.Num(2))))
    print(eval(Expr.Mul(Expr.Num(2), Expr.Num(3))))
}
```

